# Exercise: Detecting Peer Close
#
# The Bool returned by `net.tcp.read` is necessary but not sufficient
# for handling a stream. There are three real outcomes:
#
#   ( "...data..." true  )   — got bytes, keep going
#   ( ""           true  )   — peer closed cleanly (EOF)
#   ( ""           false )   — hard error (RST, kernel error, ...)
#
# Production code has to handle all three. The Bool covers only the
# third one. The clean-EOF case is success with an empty string —
# read it as "the other side hung up." Loop forever on a peer-close
# without this check and you have an infinite spin-read.
#
# This exercise: the connector connects and *immediately closes*
# without writing anything. The server accepts, then reads — which
# returns `("" true)`. Your task is to distinguish that from a real
# read of data, and from a hard error.
#
# Your task: write the read with proper three-way handling.
#   - Bool true  + empty data  → peer closed, assert that string.length is 0
#   - Bool true  + non-empty   → unexpected data, fail the test
#   - Bool false               → hard error, fail the test
# When done, delete the marker line below.

# I AM NOT DONE

: connector ( -- )
    "127.0.0.1" 18264 net.tcp.connect
    [ net.tcp.close drop ]
    [ drop ]
    if
;

: test-close ( -- )
    18264 net.tcp.listen
    [ # ( server )
      [ connector ] strand.spawn drop
      dup net.tcp.accept
      [ # ( server client )
        # Replace this block with your three-way handling.
        # End with stack ( server client ) for the cleanup below.
        false test.assert                # placeholder

        # Do not edit below this line
        net.tcp.close drop
        net.tcp.close drop
      ]
      [ drop net.tcp.close drop ]
      if
    ]
    [ drop ]
    if
;
